 | R117 | inform | The real pickup spec, the potion-cap town rule, and the inventory cleanse | User check-in at the gate: (1) a full pickup whitelist — potion caps (2 heal / 2 mana / 4 rejuv), flawless+perfect gems/skulls, all runes, unique charms, rare+unique jewels/rings/amulets, named unique/set/socketed bases, and ~30 PD2 quest-and-map items — with `filters/local/eqN-All-In-One.filter` as the display/vocabulary reference and "easily amendable by a human" as the format bar; (2) the town loop should drink only potions in excess of 2 per type, keep up to 2 of each in inventory, and never stash potions; (3) a new accidental-pickup recovery: CTRL+right-click drops items, an "inventory cleanse" whitelist-check runs only when no enemies are near, queued after a failed pickup. | Recorded; clarifications batched as R118. Supersedes parts of R46 Q3 (simple pickit) and R75 (drink-all-excess, rejuvs-to-materials). |
 | R118 | decision | Pickup-spec confirmation batch (Q1–Q5) | Q1 potion-cap semantics · Q2 rejuv end-state · Q3 rule format · Q4 discovery drills for item ids + socket stat · Q5 cleanse details (town handling, drop-at-feet) | **All five answered.** Q1: belt first (4 rejuvs), then up to 2 of EACH type in inventory, all excess drunk "just like other potions". Q2: never stash potions, drink all excess including rejuvs — retires R75's rejuvs-to-materials. Q3: TOML stays. Q4: approved. Q5: yes — dropping junk is always advisable as overflow insurance; the town preamble drops rather than stashes non-whitelisted items. Built same-day as phase 05b (sim-side); T38/T39 drills pending → R119. |
 | R119 | execute | Run the T38 + T39 discovery drills (bridge) | Start the elevated bridge, then run T38 (socket stat) and T39 (item ids). Both read-only; the bot sends nothing but its own chat. | **T38 PASS** (see R124). T39 not yet run — redesigned first around the chat return channel (R120–R123), which landed between the request and the run. |
+| R130 | inform | "We can try again — just make sure to ctrl+right click" | User, on T43's abort: a plain right-click drinks a potion, so check the modifier is really being held. | **Right instinct, wrong culprit — and the retry proved it.** T44 tried four ctrl spellings on a GEM (whose unmodified right-click is a no-op, so a failure would cost nothing and be visible, unlike a potion where both hypotheses end in a drunk potion). The FIRST variant worked — the original `VK_CONTROL` 0x11 with a one-frame settle — and a ground read then found T43's "lost" antidote lying at (5890, 5735). It had dropped correctly all along. **T43's verification was the defect**: it read the ground ONCE, immediately, and a just-dropped item takes a moment to enter the unit table. Fixed by polling; the speculative `VK_LCONTROL` is kept only as a documented alternative with a note not to reach for it. Same shape as the P3 calibration crisis (R86): the instrument was wrong, not the game. |
+| R129 | decision | P6 stage A — go | User: "OK, lets do P6 stage A. We leave B for tomorrow. After that, we do a yona review, and ideally get ready to push and finish for the night." | **PASSED** (via T43's audit + T44's verification). The ctrl+right-click drop is live-verified by effect, the last untested input path in the bot. T43's whitelist audit also did its job: of 12 carried items it would keep 8 and drop 4 — the two tomes, a misc item, and a **magic grand charm** — which is correct for an accidental pickup and would have been unwelcome for a charm, exactly the distinction the startup-baseline guard (R128) exists to protect. No combat, no travel, nothing lost. |
 | R128 | decision | The R127 option-A review table | Presented all 53 remaining names with proposed code, live-read kind, confidence and evidence (`pickit-id-proposal.md`); asked for corrections on the 7 low-confidence elite armours and the 6 recipe-triangle inferences. | **"Looks right to me. The ones you didn't find likely can't drop (can only be crafted)."** All 44 proposals accepted; vocabulary now **105 names, zero pending**, and the inventory cleanse gate opened by itself. The craft-only remark did real work: those 9 names are retired rather than left pending (a pickup rule for an item that cannot drop can never fire, and leaving them pending would have held the cleanse disabled forever) — **and it exposed a hole**: an item that can only be crafted can never teach the whitelist its id from a live pickup, so one sitting in the inventory would look exactly like junk. Closed with a startup-baseline guard: the cleanse now refuses to drop anything the bot was already carrying when it started, because the feature exists to clean up the bot's OWN accidents. 584 tests. |
 | R127 | decision | How to resolve the last ~45 names | (A) a table to eyeball, (B) extract the MPQ string tables, (C) leave pending and resolve opportunistically. | **A, with C as fallback.** A worked: the filter names items through their CUBE RECIPES — each rune's tooltip reads "3x Hel + Key = Io", and the quest items name each other ("Cube this item with: Black Soul Stone & Prime Evil Soul" identifies the third by elimination). 44 of 53 resolved from that plus block position; B never needed. |
 | R126 | verify | T39 run 1 — ABORTED by the user, and worth more than a pass | User: the drill demanded "very precise nomenclature I'm not familiar with" — it rejected `hel rune`, `rune`, `hel_rune` and `Perfect Sapphire` — and they do not have many of the listed items to hand. Asked for a redesign, and offered to help if some information cannot be got from the game or filter files. | **The abort log caught a real bug in MY work, in its first 30 seconds.** The user's Hel rune read kind **713 = `r15s`** and their Perfect Sapphire **690 = `gpbs`**, while the R125 join had bound those names to the classic `r15` (639) and `gpb` (586). **PD2 carries two parallel item families** — the classic records and an `s`-suffixed set (`r01s`-`r33s` at 699-731, `gzvs`-`skzs` at 685-698) — and the `s` family is what drops. So ~49 of the 53 auto-resolved ids were wrong. This is exactly the systematic mis-pairing the spot-check existed to catch, and asking for a rune and a gem FIRST is why it cost seconds. Fixes: `ItemTable` now maps a name to a TUPLE of kinds (both records bound, since an item is one or the other); vocabulary regenerated with the two live ids asserted at generation time; regression tests added. Second defect, purely UX and also mine: the drill accepted only PENDING names, so a correct answer for an already-known item was rejected as "not on the wanted list" — the question was open but the answer space was not. |
diff --git a/pd2bot/input.py b/pd2bot/input.py
index 2a7946f..68d995f 100644
--- a/pd2bot/input.py
+++ b/pd2bot/input.py
@@ -45,7 +45,24 @@ _KEY_UP = 0x0002
 # owns the SendInput plumbing; the *meaning* of a key (which skill, which
 # belt column) lives with the caller's config, not here.
 VK_SHIFT = 0x10
-VK_CONTROL = 0x11  # ctrl+right-click drops an inventory item (R117)
+# Ctrl+right-click drops an inventory item (R117). VK_CONTROL is the one
+# the client honours — verified live in T44, first variant, gem dropped
+# and found on the ground.
+#
+# VK_LCONTROL exists only as a documented alternative, and the story is
+# worth keeping: T43 concluded ctrl was being ignored, because the item
+# it dropped could not be found on the floor. That was wrong. The drop
+# had worked; T43 read the ground ONCE, immediately, and a just-dropped
+# item takes a moment to enter the unit table. The antidote it "lost" was
+# later found lying exactly where it fell.
+#
+# So the bug was in the instrument, not the game — the same shape as the
+# whole P3 calibration crisis (R86). A verification that polls would have
+# passed first time, and the speculative per-side keycode below was never
+# needed. Keep it for the day some other client really does read key
+# state per-side; do not reach for it before a poll-based test says so.
+VK_CONTROL = 0x11
+VK_LCONTROL = 0xA2
 VK_F1, VK_F2, VK_F3, VK_F4, VK_F5, VK_F6 = 0x70, 0x71, 0x72, 0x73, 0x74, 0x75
 VK_1, VK_2, VK_3, VK_4 = 0x31, 0x32, 0x33, 0x34
 # NPC dialogs are keyboard-navigable: arrows move the highlight, Enter
